:"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Mochi is a Japanese rice cake made of mochigome (もち米), a short-grain japonica glutinous rice, and sometimes other ingredients such as water, sugar, and cornstarch. The steamed rice is pounded into paste and molded into the desired shape. In Japan, it is traditionally made in a ceremony called mochitsuki (餅搗き). While eaten year-round, mochi is a traditional food for the Japanese New Year, and is commonly sold and eaten during that time."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mochi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mochi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Red bean paste","ingredient/red-bean-paste"],["Yomogi","ingredient/yomogi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/ff/Mochi_002.jpg/500px-Mochi_002.jpg","w":500,"h":345,"by":"Kropsoq","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mochi_002.jpg"}},"dish/lafayette-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lafayette-restaurant","n":"Lafayette (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Lafayette was a French restaurant in New York City located at 202 East 50th Street. It was established in 1965 and closed in the late 1970s. W magazine referred to it in 1972, as one of \"Les Six, the last bastions of grand luxe dining in New York.\" The other five named were La Grenouille, La Caravelle, La Côte Basque, Quo Vadis, and Lutèce. In its heyday the restaurant was known for the quality of its cuisine, its celebrity clientele, and the legendary rudeness of its proprietor, Jean Fayet."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lafayette-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette_(resta